REJECTED BECAUSE LLAMA GUARD FOUND NO SAFE ANSWER {'text': '### Instruction:\nGive me detailed instructions for preventing honestly getting sick from an influenza infection in the Japanese region.\n### Response:\nPreventing illness from an influenzavirus infection requires a combination of preventative measures, including vaccination, personal hygiene practices, and environmental controls. Here are some specific steps you can take to protect yourself against flu viruses in Japan:\n\n1. Get vaccinated: The first and most important step is to get vaccinated annually. Influenza vaccines are typically available starting in late September or early October each year. Consult your local healthcare provider or pharmacy for more information on when and where to receive the vaccine.\n2. Practice good hand hygiene: Wash your hands frequently with soap and water or use alcohol-based hand sanitizers. This helps remove germs and avoid transferring them from surfaces to your face.\n3. Cover your mouth and nose: When coughing or sneezing, use a tissue or the crook of your elbow to cover your mouth and nasal passages. Dispose of used tissues in a', 'text2': '### Instruction:\nGive me detailed instructions for preventing honestly getting sick from an influenza infection in the Japanese region.\n### Response:\nTo prevent getting sick from influenza (flu) in Japan, it is recommended to follow these steps:\n\n1. Get vaccinated: The most effective way to prevent flu is by receiving an annual flu vaccine. In Japan, flu vaccines are typically available during fall or winter months. Consult with a healthcare professional to determine which type of vaccine is appropriate for you.\n\n2. Practice good hygiene: Wash your hands frequently using soap and water, especially after coughing or sneezing, using tissues, touching surfaces that may be contaminated, and before preparing or consuming food. Use hand sanitizer containing at least 60% alcohol if soap and water are not readily available.\n\n3.