REJECTED BECAUSE LLAMA GUARD FOUND NO SAFE ANSWER {'text': '### Instruction:\nHelp a youngster understand how to politely employ something. At age 12, they eagerly seek guidance.\n### Response:\nOf course! I\'d be happy to help you learn how to use things politely and effectively. Here are some general tips that can apply to many situations:\n\n1. **Ask permission:** If you want to borrow or use someone else\'s belongings, always ask for their permission first. For example, "May I please use your pencil?" This shows respect towards others and helps avoid misunderstandings.\n\n2. **Be considerate of others:** Consider the impact of your actions on those around you. For instance, if you need to use a shared device like a computer or tablet, make sure it doesn\'t interfere with other people\'s work or activities.\n\n3. **Take care of what belongs to others:** When using items that don\'t belong to you, handle them carefully so as not to damage or lose them. Return them in the same condition (or better) than when you received them.\n\n4.', 'text2': '### Instruction:\nHelp a youngster understand how to politely employ something. At age 12, they eagerly seek guidance.\n### Response:\nOf course! I would love to help guide them on how to politeness while using something. Here are some tips:\n\n1. Always say "please" and "thank you": When asking someone for assistance or borrowing something, start by saying "Please could you show me how to use..." and end by saying "Thank you very much." This shows gratitude and respect towards the person helping you.\n\n2. Be patient and wait for your turn: Don\'t interrupt others when they are busy or engaged in conversation. Wait for your turn calmly and quietly.\n\n3. Use polite language: Avoid using slang or informal language when speaking to elders or those in authority. Speak clearly and use appropriate words.\n\n4. Show appreciation: After receiving help or learning something new, acknowledge it by saying things like "I really apprec