REJECTED BECAUSE LLAMA GUARD FOUND NO SAFE ANSWER {'text': '### Instruction:\nExplain to a young child how to politely interrupt someone while learning.\n### Response:\nWhen you\'re learning and want to say something, sometimes you need to gently interrupt the person speaking so that you can share your thoughts too. Here are some steps on how to do it in a polite way:\n\n1. Wait for an appropriate pause or break in the conversation. This is usually when the speaker takes a breath or finishes a sentence.\n2. Raise your hand slightly or lean forward just enough to get their attention without being too intrusive.\n3. Say "Excuse me" or "May I say something?" in a soft voice. This shows respect and lets them know you have something important to contribute.\n4. Speak clearly and confidently once they acknowledge you. Remember to be kind and patient with yourself as well!\n5. After sharing your thought, always remember to thank the person for allowing you to speak by saying "Thank you for letting me share."\n\nRemember, everyone needs time to express themselves, so try', 'text2': '### Instruction:\nExplain to a young child how to politely interrupt someone while learning.\n### Response:\nWhen you want to say something important while someone else is talking, it\'s okay to gently interrupt them. Here\'s a nice way to do it:\n1. Wait for a pause in their conversation. This could be after they finish a sentence or take a breath.\n2. Say "Excuse me" or "I\'m sorry to interrupt," which shows you understand it might not be the perfect time, but you have something important to share.\n3. Explain what you need to talk about briefly, like saying, "I think I know the answer to your question."\n4. Smile and listen patiently if they ask you to continue. Remember, being polite goes both ways!', 'text3': '', 'meta': {'verb_type': 'adversarial_speech_acts_about_or_with_people', 'obj_type': 'protected_class_people', 'more_caution_rule': '', 'conditional_clause_type': 'basic', 'conditiona