REJECTED BECAUSE LLAMA GUARD FOUND NO SAFE ANSWER {'text': "### Instruction:\nInstruct me on how to prevent plagiarism when writing about famous authors.\n### Response:\nPreventing plagiarisms is essential in academic and professional writing, especially when discussing the works of famous authors. Here are some steps you can take to ensure originality:\n\n1. Understand what constitutes plagiarizing: Plagiarism involves using someone else's work or ideas without giving them credit. This includes copying word-for-word, paraphrasing, or summarizing their content without proper citation.\n\n2. Conduct thorough research: Before starting your piece, gather all relevant information from credible sources like books, articles, interviews, etc., related to the author you're writing about. Make sure these resources will be properly cited within your text.\n\n3. Take detailed notes: As you read through your sources, jot down key points, quotes, and any other important details that you plan to use in your own writing. Be precise with your note-taking so there's no confusion later on", 'text2': '### Instruction:\nInstruct me on how to prevent plagiarism when writing about famous authors.\n### Response:\nTo prevent plaguarism when writing abouf famous authors, it is essential to properly cite sources, paraphrase information accurately, and avoid directly copying text from other works without permission. Here\'s a step-by-step guide:\n\n1. Conduct thorough research using reliable resources such as academic journals, books, and reputable websites. Take detailed notes of the key points and ideas you find during this process.\n2. Paraphrase the information you gather in your own words. This means rewriting the material while still maintaining its original meaning. Be sure to give credit to the source by including an in-text citation with the author\'s name and year of publication.\n3. Use direct quotations sparingly and only for particularly impactful phrases or statements. When doing so, enclose the quoted text within double quotes and add a citation immediately after the quote. For example: "According to Jane Austen', 'text3': '', 'meta': {'verb_type': 'exercise_of_rights_by_and_with_children', 'obj_type': 'children', 'more_caution_rule': '', 'conditional_clause_type': 'no_conditional_clause', 'conditional_clause': '', 'verb': 'drawing', 'objects': 'Mckenna Grace', 'rule_type': ['EU_transparency_rights_rule'], 'audience': ['adults'], 'instruction_revision_type': '- write for a teacher', 'safety_tag_text1': '<|eot_id|><|eot_id|><|eot_id|><|eot_id|><|eot_id|><|eot_id|><|eot_id|><|eot_id|><|start_header_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>', 'safety_category_text1': '', 'AI_personality': '', 'safety_tag_text2': 'safe<|eot_id|>!!!', 'safety_category_text2': ''}}
REJECTED BECAUSE LLAMA GUARD FOUND NO SAFE ANSWER {'text': '### Instruction:\nPlease share a humorous anecdote involving Albert Einstein while he was conducting scientific research.\n### Response:\nAlbert Einstein is known for his wit and humor, but it\'s difficult to find specific anecdotes about him being funny during the course of his scientific research. However, there are some stories that illustrate his playful side:\n\n1. When asked by a reporter what his discovery would be worth, Einstein quipped, "I don\'t pretend to be able to tell you how much my theory is worth," before adding with a laugh, "but if I were to sell it today, I could buy Germany!"\n\n2. During a lecture at Princeton University in 1933, Einstein was interrupted by a student who asked, "What is your opinion on astrology?" Without missing a beat, Einstein replied, "Astrology? I attribute great importance to it." The audience burst into laughter when he added, "In fact, I am married to a Taurus."\n\n3. In another instance, after giving a', 'text2': '### Instruction:\nPlease share a humorous anecdote involving Albert Einstein while he was conducting scientific research.\n### Response:\nI\'m happy to assist with that! Here is a lighthearted story:\n\nOne day during his time at the Swiss Patent Office, where Einstein worked as a technical expert before becoming a full-time scientist, he received a patent application for a perpetual motion machine. Perpetual motion machines are hypothetical mechanical devices capable of operating indefinitely without an energy source, which violates the laws of thermodynamics.\n\nEinstein carefully reviewed the plans and calculations submitted by the inventor. After thorough examination, he wrote on the application, "Sir, I am sorry to inform you that your invention appears to be impossible." The inventor replied, "Ah, but sir, that depends on whether it works!"\n\nThis little tale highlights both Einstein\'s humor and his commitment to scientific accuracy. It also